#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""themectl -- the desktop's theme generator.

One theme file (themes/<name>.json: a light/dark pair of colour tokens, shared by the shell, the lock, the mockups and
this tool) in, the files and settings of the desktop's programs out. It replaces ~/.config/HyprV/waybar/scripts/
theme-switch.py, whose real fault was not any one target but `stdout=DEVNULL, stderr=DEVNULL`: two of its targets were
dead for months and nothing said so. Here EVERY target prints `ok` or `FAIL <reason>` and the exit status is the number
of FAILs.

    themectl apply                  make the desktop match ~/.config/theme/state.json
    themectl set <theme> [mode]     choose a theme (and mode), then apply
    themectl toggle                 light <-> dark, then apply
    themectl next                   the next theme (alphabetical); with one theme it says so and does nothing
    themectl diff [theme [mode]]    what apply would change; writes nothing
    themectl list [--json]          themes, the current state, the targets (--json: one object, for a program)
    themectl doctor                 is the machine ready: programs, include lines, assets, templates, and per pair which preferred theme
                                    package is missing and what it falls back to (writes nothing)
    themectl packages               the install list for the preferred theme packages that are missing (installs nothing)
    themectl install [--dry-run]    copy the repo's themes to ~/.config/theme/themes, and its btop themes and fastfetch logos to their
                                    apps' directories (never over a newer file); carry a pair the repo renamed over (RENAMES: no ghost of the
                                    old name); set yazi up for the wallpapers once (the plugin, init.lua, yazi.toml: tools/theme/yazi/)
    themectl migrate [--dry-run]    make the one-time edits of the apps' own configs that `doctor` asks for (originals kept; see MIGRATE)
    themectl restore [target ...]   put back the originals themectl kept (~/.config/theme/backup); `restore migrate` is the edits above
    themectl wallpaper set <image>  ADD a picture to the CURRENT theme and mode's list (shell.jsonc theme.wallpapers.<theme>.<mode>, by an edit of
                                    its text: comments and layout stay) and show it now; a notification with a Revert button says so (yazi's
                                    "Set as wallpaper" entry runs this); exactly one image, else refused
    themectl wallpaper remove <img> take a picture out of that list (the one on screen: the next is drawn; the last one: refused) -- yazi's
                                    "Remove from wallpapers", offered on the listed pictures only
    themectl wallpaper list [t [m]] a slot's pictures, its current pick and what is still to come in this round
    themectl resolve [...]          the resolved tokens of a theme as JSON (tools/dev/themectl_resolver_parity.sh)

Options for apply / set / toggle / next / diff:  --only a,b  --skip a,b  (target names; see `list`). The `lares` target (`quickshell` is its old name and still works) is what
writes state.json: `set eva dark --only btop` renders btop's dark theme and records nothing.

THE FILES.
  ~/.config/theme/state.json         {"theme": "eva", "mode": "light"} -- the only file a toggle writes; Theme.qml watches it
  ~/.config/theme/themes/<n>.json    the themes (L1); falls back to <shell>/themes/<n>.json until `install`
  ~/.config/quickshell/config/shell.jsonc   read for ONE thing: "theme": { "wallpapers": { "<theme>": { "light": "<path>" | ["<path>", ...], "dark": ... } },
                                     "wallpaperFit": "cover" } -- the wallpaper target (hyprpaper). Nothing else of it is read.
  ~/.local/state/theme/              wallpaper-bag.json (each slot's shuffle bag and current pick), wallpapers-active.txt (the current slot's
                                     pictures, for yazi's plugin)
  ~/.config/windows/compose.yaml     read for ONE thing, the volume mounted at /shared: the windows-vm target (windows/INSTALL.md). Nothing starts or talks to the VM.
  tools/theme/targets.json           per target: a template, an output file, a reload command   (~/.config/theme/targets.json
  tools/theme/templates/*            overrides it, ~/.config/theme/templates/ the templates)
  tools/theme/btop/*, fastfetch/*    what a link target ships (`shipped`): btop's custom themes, the logos; copied next to the link
  ~/.config/theme/backup/            the first time themectl owns a file, the original goes here (manifest.json)

THE THEME FILE (the grammar the shell's Theme.qml reads too -- modules/common/theme/ThemeResolve.js is the other copy of
the resolver, and tools/dev/themectl_resolver_parity.sh holds them to the same answers token by token):
  {"name": "eva", "common": {...}, "light": {...}, "dark": {...}, "assets": {"light": {...}, "dark": {...}}}
  An asset that names an installed theme (gtkTheme, iconTheme, vscodeTheme) may be a list, best first: the first one that is installed
  is used, and with none installed the LAST is used as it always was. tools/theme/packages.json says what installs each name.
  A key of the mode block wins over `common`, which wins over L0 (the defaults in Theme.qml's `defaults`). Every key is
  optional. A value is "#RGB" | "#RRGGBB" | "#AARRGGBB" | "@name" (an alias) | "@name lighten|darken|alpha n"
  (lighten/darken: HSL lightness +/- n, alpha: set the alpha to n; n in 0..1) | anything else (a number, a font name:
  passed through). Cycles, unknown names and bad recipes are errors; the key then falls back to L0.
  Ten tokens exist for the programs alone and a theme may leave them out: FALLBACKS (below) says what each one then is.
  tools/theme/pairgen.py made the pairs after eva and everbox from their source palettes (its header says how).

NEVER AGAINST THE REAL DESKTOP IN A TEST: tools/dev/themectl_sandbox.py gives every test run a HOME of its own, a PATH of
stubs and the keyfile gsettings backend.

Exit status: the number of FAILs (0..99); 100 = refused before any target ran (unknown theme, bad state, ...).
"""
